🍽️ Cuisine Classifier

Predicts a recipe's cuisine from its ingredient list using a TF-IDF + Naive Bayes pipeline, deployed as an interactive Streamlit app.

How it works
Training (train_model.py) — loads ~40,000 labeled recipes (20 cuisines) from train.json, joins each recipe's ingredients into a single string, and fits a TfidfVectorizer → MultinomialNB pipeline via scikit-learn.
Inference (app.py) — a Streamlit UI where a user types ingredients in free text; the app cleans the input, predicts probabilities across all 20 cuisines, and shows the top 3 with confidence scores.
Explainability — a lightweight method surfaces which entered ingredients had the strongest TF-IDF weight for the predicted class, so predictions aren't a black box.
Results
67.9% held-out accuracy across all 20 cuisine classes
Trained/evaluated on an 80/20 split of ~40K recipes
Dataset is significantly imbalanced (7,838 Italian vs. 467 Brazilian recipes), so accuracy is reported on the full multi-class problem rather than a balanced subset
Tech stack

Python · scikit-learn (TF-IDF, Naive Bayes) · pandas · Streamlit · joblib

Running locally
bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
python train_model.py      # trains and saves cuisine_classifier.pkl
streamlit run app.py       # launches the UI at localhost:8501
Files
File	Purpose
train_model.py	Trains the TF-IDF + Naive Bayes pipeline and saves the model
app.py	Streamlit UI for interactive predictions
train.json / test.json	Recipe dataset (ingredients + cuisine labels)
cuisine_classifier.pkl	Trained, serialized model
Possible extensions
Swap Naive Bayes for a gradient-boosted or transformer-based classifier to push past ~68% accuracy
Address class imbalance with class weighting or oversampling
Expose ingredient-level SHAP values for richer explainability 
